    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The 50 MSPS constraint is the binding one and it forces parallelism. iterative's 1 micro-rotation/cycle schedule cannot reach 50 MSPS with the N>=13 iterations needed for max_abs_err <= 2^-12, so it gets only a small probe. unrolled_k is the only family here that can plausibly meet both constraints: with k micro-rotations/cycle the schedule is ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, so N~13-16 and k~4-8 give 5-7 cycles and a required Fmax of ~250-350 MHz. Accuracy depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding, so the ranges are chosen to straddle the 2^-12 boundary (W>=14, N>=~13) and to extend to the high-accuracy end that earns hypervolume, while k spans the Fmax/cycles trade-off so the search can locate the min-power feasible corner (small W, small N, smallest k that still hits 50 MSPS). 90/10 split puts the budget where the trade-off is and keeps a minimal negative-control probe on iterative."
